// No sentence in this file may assert what a regulation requires, permits or does not require.
//
// Stage 3 withheld two German statements on that rule, and a later decision withdrew their English and
// Polish twins, because the reason a statement needs a qualified reviewer — nobody here is one — does
// not change with the language it is written in. Both withholdings were scoped to statements about the
// *state* of the legislation. Neither touched statements about what the legislation *demands*, and two
// of those were still live on `/connect` in all three locales at 1.0: a heading asserting that PPWR does
// not require SAP, and a sentence asserting that Regulation (EU) 2025/40 does not require TRACES.
//
// Both are very probably true. That is not the test. A negative claim about a regulation is still a
// claim about a regulation, a reader may act on it, and the only person who can sign it is one we have
// not appointed.
//
// Deleting them was rejected: a reader who sees `TRACES / IMSOC — Discovery` in the integration list
// needs to know what TRACES is and what we do with it, and someone weighing OpenPPWR against an
// SAP-shaped procurement needs to know the product does not need SAP. Both facts survive, restated as
// facts about this software — what Community imports, what an assessment uses as evidence, what the
// product reads and writes — because a claim about our own product is one we can be held to and can
// check. Where the regulatory question genuinely matters to the reader, the copy now says that it is a
// regulatory question and that this page does not answer it, which is the honest position rather than a
// quieter version of the same assertion.

// A contact address is a promise that someone reads that mailbox, so two surfaces must never name
// different ones for the same purpose.
//
// The security page and the imprint gave `security@openppwr.eu` with published triage targets, while
// `SECURITY.md` — the policy that ships to every self-hoster — gave the company address and declined any
// response target at all. A researcher reading the website therefore wrote to an address the shipped
// policy did not name and expected a response it refused to promise. The privacy notice had the same
// shape of defect: it sent data subject requests to the company address while the imprint gave
// `privacy@openppwr.eu`.
//
// Both are resolved towards the dedicated addresses, because those are the ones the owner created for
// these purposes and the ones the imprint publishes; `SECURITY.md` was changed to match rather than the
// other way round. Dropping the company address was rejected — a researcher or a data subject who
// already holds it would find it dead — so both surfaces now say plainly that either address reaches the
// same people, instead of leaving a reader to work it out.

// The processor list does not claim to be complete, and must not read as though it does.
//
// An explicit completeness claim was removed from this page earlier because nobody had confirmed the
// list was exhaustive in a form a lawyer would accept. The opening sentence then said "These are our
// processors for openppwr.eu", which a reader takes as the same claim in weaker grammar — the definite
// article does the work the removed sentence used to. It now names the list as the processors we use,
// says in as many words that it is not offered as exhaustive, and says why, which is the state the
// removal was meant to leave the page in.
